            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Create a Disaster Preparedness Plan for your house or place of work. Know what you and your family are going to do before the earthquake happens. Form your plan together and go over it on a regular basis. The most important first step is understanding what to do the moment an earthquake hits.<ref>http://seismo.berkeley.edu/outreach/be_prepared.html</ref> This plan needs to:[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*'''Teach everyone how to signal for help if trapped.''' Rescuers searching collapsed buildings will be listening for sounds, so try knocking three times repeatedly or blow an emergency whistle if you have access to one.<ref>http://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/2005/15/</ref>",
                    "#*'''Practice until it is second nature.''' Practice this plan often—you only have a few seconds to make adjustments in a real earthquake.<ref>https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes</ref>",
                    "#Write down and share emergency contact lists. This should include everyone in your home, office, etc. You need to know who must be accounted for and how to get in touch with them if they can't be found. In addition to normal contact information, ask each person to provide and emergency contact as well.<ref>http://emergency.cdc.gov/disasters/earthquakes/prepared.asp</ref> You should also include:[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Prepare a disaster supply kit, and inform the entire household of its location. Earthquakes can trap people in their homes for days at a time in the worst-case scenario, so you need everything for survival in the house.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Water purification tablets<ref>http://emergency.cdc.gov/disasters/earthquakes/prepared.asp</ref>",
                    "#*Rope ladder<ref>http://earthquakecountry.org/step3/</ref>",
                    "#*Games, cards, toys, and writing materials<ref>http://earthquakecountry.org/step3/</ref>",
                    "#*Fasten all shelves securely to the walls.<ref>http://emergency.cdc.gov/disasters/earthquakes/prepared.asp</ref>",
                    "#*Use an invisible nylon cord to secure tall, heavy items that can topple over to the wall. Place an eye screw in the wall, and tie the thread around the object (such as a vase) and then tie it to the eye screw.<ref>http://www.redcross.org/prepare/disaster/earthquake</ref>",
                    "#Remove or secure hanging objects from above seating and sleeping areas. Heavy pictures, light fixtures, and mirrors should be hung away from beds, couches, and anywhere that someone might sit. Conventional picture hooks will not hold pictures during an earthquake, but they are easy to fix - simply push the hook closed, or use a filler material to fill the gap between the hook and its backing.<ref>http://emergency.cdc.gov/disasters/earthquakes/prepared.asp</ref>[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 17.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Create a Disaster Preparedness Plan for your house or place of work. Know what you and your family are going to do before the earthquake happens. Form your plan together and go over it on a regular basis. The most important first step is understanding what to do the moment an earthquake hits.<ref>http://seismo.berkeley.edu/outreach/be_prepared.html</reF> This plan needs to:[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*'''Teach everyone how to signal for help if trapped.''' Rescuers searching collapsed buildings will be listening for sounds, so try knocking three times repeatedly or blow an emergency whistle if you have access to one.<Ref> http://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/2005/15/ </ref>",
                    "#*'''Practice until it is second nature.''' Practice this plan often -- you only have a few seconds to make adjustments in a real earthquake.<ref>https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes</ref>",
                    "#Write down and share emergency contact lists. This should include everyone in your home, office, etc. You need to know who must be accounted for and how to get in touch with them if they can't be found. In addition to normal contact information, ask each person to provide and emergency contact as well.<reF>http://emergency.cdc.gov/disasters/earthquakes/prepared.asp</ref> You should also include:[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Prepare a disaster supply kit, and inform the entire household of its location. Earthquakes can trap people in their homes for days at a time in the worst case scenario, so you need everything for survival in the house.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Water purification tablets<reF>http://emergency.cdc.gov/disasters/earthquakes/prepared.asp</ref>",
                    "#*Rope ladder<reF>http://earthquakecountry.org/step3/</ref>",
                    "#*Games, cards, toys, and writing materials<reF>http://earthquakecountry.org/step3/</ref>",
                    "#*Fasten all shelves securely to the walls.<reF>http://emergency.cdc.gov/disasters/earthquakes/prepared.asp</ref>",
                    "#*Use an invisible nylon cord to secure tall, heavy items that can topple over to the wall. Place an eye screw in the wall, and tie the thread around the object (such as a vase) and then tie it to the eye screw.<reF>http://www.redcross.org/prepare/disaster/earthquake</ref>",
                    "#Remove or secure hanging objects from above seating and sleeping areas. Heavy pictures, light fixtures, and mirrors should be hung away from beds, couches, and anywhere that someone might sit. Conventional picture hooks will not hold pictures during an earthquake, but they are easy to fix - simply push the hook closed, or use a filler material to fill the gap between the hook and its backing.<reF>http://emergency.cdc.gov/disasters/earthquakes/prepared.asp</ref>[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 17.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
