        {
            "id": "25671456",
            "user": "Hapayne",
            "timestamp": "18:56, 29 November 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,428 bytes)",
            "change": -880,
            "summary": "",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*If you cannot attend a class, purchase basic first aid books and put them with each stash of emergency supplies in the house. Having a first aid kit is highly recommended.",
                    "#Try to develop routes and methods for getting home after an earthquake has occurred. There is no way to know what time of day an earthquake might strike, you may be at work, at school, on a bus, or in a train when one strikes. It is highly likely that you will need to know several ways to get home since roads and bridges will likely be obstructed for long periods of time. Note any potentially dangerous structures, like bridges, and figure out a route around them if need be.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Prepare a disaster supply kit, and inform the entire household of its location. Earthquakes can trap people in their homes for days at a time in the worst case scenario, so you need everything for survival in the house.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*You should also purchase glow-sticks, matches, and candles as backup options.",
                    "#*Anti-diarrhea medication (essential to fight dehydration in an emergency)",
                    "#*Crowbar",
                    "#*Rope ladder<reF>http://earthquakecountry.org/step3/</ref>",
                    "#*Games, cards, toys, and writing materials<reF>http://earthquakecountry.org/step3/</ref>",
                    "#*Scanner (It will be helpful to hear outside information on a scanner)",
                    "#*Use brackets to attach wall units, bookcases, and other tall furniture to wall studs. Standard steel brackets are fine and easy to apply.",
                    "#Place breakable items in closed cabinets that have latches. Lock or latch them up so that the cabinet doors cannot fly open. Use poster tack/plastic putty to keep ornaments, figurines, and glassware adhered to shelves and mantelpieces.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove or secure hanging objects from above seating and sleeping areas. Heavy pictures, light fixtures, and mirrors should be hung away from beds, couches, and anywhere that someone might sit. Conventional picture hooks will not hold pictures during an earthquake, but they are easy to fix - simply push the hook closed, or use a filler material to fill the gap between the hook and its backing.<reF>http://emergency.cdc.gov/disasters/earthquakes/prepared.asp</ref>[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Other alternatives include buying special art hooks and ensuring that heavy paintings have adequate, strong hooks and cord.",
                    "#Check that your house is up-to-date with earthquake protections. A landlord or your local zoning board can help you determine this. If you have any deep cracks in the ceiling or foundations, repair these immediately. You might need to consult an expert if there are signs of structural weakness. Make sure your foundation is properly braced and that all modern laws and building regulations are being complied with.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Evaluate your electrical wiring, electrical appliances, and gas connections. Do any repairs if needed. During an earthquake, faulty fittings and wiring can become a potential fire hazard. When securing appliances, be sure not to drill holes in them. You can use existing holes, or make loops from leather, that can be glued onto an appliance.",
                    "#Work with your community to prepare for earthquakes together. If there aren't civic groups present in your area focused on earthquake preparedness, work on creating one. Here you can share resources, find meeting points, and provide support for one another in the case of an earthquake.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 19.jpg|center]]",
                    "*If an earthquake strikes, make sure all gas lines are completely tightened. And '''do not''' turn any lights on after an earthquake.",
                    "*Consider keeping a pair of shoes and flashlight under your bed. Similar items should be kept at your desk at work or school (for work, keep a pair of comfortable walking shoes ready).",
                    "*Plastic putty. This can be purchased from hardware stores, stationery stores, and supermarkets. Some art and antique galleries might also have suitable putties for sale.",
                    "*Phone numbers of emergency services and shelters, in case phone service is available.",
                    "*A tool kit."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*If you cannot attend a class, purchase basic first aid books and put them with each stash of emergency supplies in the house. Having a first aid kit or highly recommended.",
                    "#Try to develop routes and methods for getting home after an earthquake has occurred. There is no way to know what time of day an earthquake might strike, you may be at work, at school, on a [[Ride a Public Transportation Bus|bus]], or in a train when one strikes. It is highly likely that you will need to know several ways to get home since roads and bridges will likely be obstructed for long periods of time. Note any potentially dangerous structures, like bridges, and figure out a route around them if need be.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Pack an Emergency Kit for the House|Prepare disaster supplies in advance, making sure the entire house knows where to find everything.]] Earthquakes can trap people in their homes for days at a time in the worst case scenario, so you need everything for survival in the house.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*You should also Purchase and use glow-sticks, matches, and candles as backup options.",
                    "#*Anti-diarrhea medication (essential to fight dehydration in an emergency_",
                    "#*Crowbar.",
                    "#*Rope ladder.<reF>http://earthquakecountry.org/step3/</ref>",
                    "#*Games, cards, kid's favorite toy, writing material, etc.<reF>http://earthquakecountry.org/step3/</ref>",
                    "#*Scanner (would be helpful to see what's going on and the police/fire dept. support)",
                    "#*Use brackets to attach wall units, [[Build a Bookshelf|bookcases]], and other tall furniture to wall studs. Standard steel brackets are fine and easy to apply.",
                    "#Place breakable items (bottles, glass, china, etc.) in closed cabinets that have latches. Lock or latch them up so that the cabinet doors cannot fly open. Use poster tack/plastic putty to keep [[Collect Christmas Tree Ornaments|ornaments]], figurines, and [[Pack and Ship Your Fine China or Glassware|glassware]] adhered to shelves and mantelpieces.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove or secure hanging objects from above seating and sleeping areas. Heavy pictures, light fixtures, and [[Build a Mirror|mirrors]] should be hung away from beds, couches, and anywhere that someone might sit. Conventional picture hooks will not hold pictures during an earthquake, but they are easy to fix - simply push the hook closed, or use a filler material to fill the gap between the hook and its backing. Other alternatives include buying special art hooks and ensuring that heavy paintings have adequate, strong hooks and cord.<reF>http://emergency.cdc.gov/disasters/earthquakes/prepared.asp</ref>[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check with a professional, your landlord, or the zoning board that your house is up-to-date with earthquake protections. f you have any deep [[Repair Cracked Ceilings With Flair|cracks in the ceiling]] or foundations, repair these immediately. You might need to consult an expert if there are signs of structural weakness. Make sure your foundation is properly braced and that all modern laws and building regulations are being complied with.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Evaluate your electrical wiring, electrical appliances, and gas connections. Do any repairs if needed. During an earthquake, faulty fittings and wiring can become a potential [[Prevent a House Fire|fire hazard]]. When securing appliances, be sure not to drill holes in them - use existing holes, or make loops from leather, etc., that can be glued onto an appliance.",
                    "#Work with your community to find meeting points, preparedness workshops, and support groups. If there aren't civic groups present in your area focused on earthquake preparedness, work on putting one together. The first step in keeping everyone safe is education.[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 19.jpg|center]]",
                    "*Read wikiHow's articles on [[Survive an Earthquake|How to survive an earthquake]], [[Deal With Being Indoors During an Earthquake|Deal with being indoors during an earthquake]], and [[React During an Earthquake|How to react during an earthquake]] for more information on what to do during an earthquake.It is always helpful to [[Be Prepared for Natural Disasters|Be Prepared for Natural Disasters]]",
                    "*Make sure all gas lines are completely tightened. And '''do not''' turn any lights on after an earthquake!",
                    "*Consider keeping a pair of shoes, a flashlight and an energy bar under your bed. Similar items should be kept at your desk at work or school (for work, keep a pair of comfortable walking shoes ready).",
                    "*If you have a pet,watch out for strange pet behavior. This can be from walking around indifferently to running about or hiding and not coming out.",
                    "*Plastic putty can be purchased from hardware stores, stationery stores, and supermarkets. Some art and antique galleries might also have suitable putties for sale.",
                    "*Clothes (enough for a minimum of 3-5 days)",
                    "*Phone numbers of emergency services and shelters, if phone service is available.",
                    "*A kit"
                ]
            }
        },
