            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "reverted_by": "Isorhythmic",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "{{nointroimg}}{{fa}}An earthquake is a very destructive natural disaster; particularly in the Pacific Rim region. In fact, scientists now believe that California is long overdue for a massive earthquake along the San Andreas fault, with a magnitude of up to 8.0.<ref>Rong-Gong Lin Il, Study shakes up scientists' view of San Andreas earthquake risk, http://www.latimes.com/news/local/la-me-earthquake-fault-20100821,0,946323.story?track=rss</ref>",
                    "#Check for hazards inside your home and begin to fix them. There are a number of specific hazards in your home that you can deal with before an earthquake occurs.{{largeimage|Prepare for an Earthquake Step 1.jpg}}",
                    "#*Fasten all shelves securely to the walls.",
                    "#*Install shatter-safe window films to protect from breaking glass. In a pinch, placing masking tape across the diagonals of your windows can keep them from shattering.",
                    "#*Use brackets to attach wall units, [[Build a Bookshelf|bookcases]], and other tall furniture to wall studs. Standard steel brackets are fine and easy to apply.",
                    "#* Place large, heavy objects on lower shelves or on the floor. They might fall during an earthquake and the less distance they have to fall, the better. You can also screw objects onto things, such as a desk."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "An earthquake is a very destructive natural disaster; particularly in the Pacific Rim region. In fact, scientists now believe that California is long overdue for a massive earthquake along the San Andreas fault, with a magnitude of up to 8.0.<ref>Rong-Gong Lin Il, Study shakes up scientists' view of San Andreas earthquake risk, http://www.latimes.com/news/local/la-me-earthquake-fault-20100821,0,946323.story?track=rss</ref>",
                    "#Check for hazards inside your home and begin to fix them. There are a number of specific hazards in your home that you can deal with before an earthquake occurs.largeimage Prepare for an Earthquake Step 1.",
                    "#Fasten all shelves securely to the walls.",
                    "#Install shatter-safe window films to protect from breaking glass. In a pinch, placing masking tape across the diagonals of your windows can keep them from shattering.",
                    "#Use brackets to attach wall units, Build a Bookshelf|bookcases]], and other tall furniture to wall studs. Standard steel brackets are fine and easy to apply.",
                    "#Place large, heavy objects on lower shelves or on the floor. They might fall during an earthquake and the less distance they have to fall, the better. You can also screw objects onto things, such as a desk."
                ]
            }
        },
