        {
            "id": "8548528",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "04:55, 9 October 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(12,749 bytes)",
            "change": 37,
            "summary": "(Enlarging and centering Steps photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Check for hazards inside your home and begin to fix them. There are a number of specific hazards in your home that you can deal with before an earthquake occurs.<br><br>[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Create a Disaster Preparedness Plan. Know what you and your family are going to do before the earthquake happens. Form your plan together and go over it on a regular basis.<br><br>[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#[[Pack an Emergency Kit for the House|Prepare disaster supplies]] in advance. The following items are important to have readily available in your home, situated in a central location:<br><br>[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Work with your community to be prepared. If there aren't civic groups present in your area focused on earthquake preparedness, work on putting one together. The first step in keeping everyone safe is education.<br><br>[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 1.jpg|right|300px]]Check for hazards inside your home and begin to fix them. There are a number of specific hazards in your home that you can deal with before an earthquake occurs.",
                    "#[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 2.jpg|right|300px]]Create a Disaster Preparedness Plan. Know what you and your family are going to do before the earthquake happens. Form your plan together and go over it on a regular basis.",
                    "#[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 3.jpg|right|300px]][[Pack an Emergency Kit for the House|Prepare disaster supplies]] in advance. The following items are important to have readily available in your home, situated in a central location:",
                    "#[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 4.jpg|right|300px]]Work with your community to be prepared. If there aren't civic groups present in your area focused on earthquake preparedness, work on putting one together. The first step in keeping everyone safe is education."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "8548527",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "04:55, 9 October 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(12,712 bytes)",
            "change": -338,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "{{fa}}[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Intro.jpg|right|300px]]An earthquake is a very dangerous natural disaster; particularly in the Pacific Rim region. In fact, scientists now believe that the highly populated Southern California area is long overdue for a massive earthquake along the San Andreas fault.<ref>Rong-Gong Lin Il, Study shakes up scientists' view of San Andreas earthquake risk, http://www.latimes.com/news/local/la-me-earthquake-fault-20100821,0,946323.story?track=rss</ref>",
                    "#[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 1.jpg|right|300px]]Check for hazards inside your home and begin to fix them. There are a number of specific hazards in your home that you can deal with before an earthquake occurs.",
                    "#*Place breakable items (bottles, glass, china, etc.) in closed cabinets that have latches. Lock or latch them up so that the cabinet doors cannot fly open. Use poster tack/plastic putty to keep [[Collect Christmas Tree Ornaments|ornaments]], figurines, and [[Pack and Ship Your Fine China or Glassware|glassware]] adhered to shelves and mantelpieces. (There are even special commercial quake putties available.)",
                    "#*Evaluate your electrical wiring, electrical appliances, and gas connections. Do any repairs if needed. During an earthquake, faulty fittings and wiring can become a potential [[Prevent a House Fire|fire hazard]]. When securing appliances, be sure not to drill holes in them - use existing holes, or make loops from leather, etc., that can be glued onto an appliance.",
                    "#[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 2.jpg|right|300px]]Create a Disaster Preparedness Plan. Know what you and your family are going to do before the earthquake happens. Form your plan together and go over it on a regular basis.",
                    "#*Try to develop routes and methods for getting home after an earthquake has occurred. Since there is no precise time of day when an earthquake might strike, you may be at work, at school, on a [[Ride a Public Transportation Bus|bus]], or in a train when one strikes. It is highly likely that you will need to know several ways to get home since roads and bridges will likely be obstructed for long periods of time.",
                    "#[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 3.jpg|right|300px]][[Pack an Emergency Kit for the House|Prepare disaster supplies]] in advance. The following items are important to have readily available in your home, situated in a central location:",
                    "#[[Image:Prepare for an Earthquake Step 4.jpg|right|300px]]Work with your community to be prepared. If there aren't civic groups present in your area focused on earthquake preparedness, work on putting one together. The first step in keeping everyone safe is education."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "{{fa}}[[Image:Paso Robles Earthquake 4744.jpg|right|250px]]An earthquake is a very dangerous natural disaster; particularly in the Pacific Rim region. In fact, scientists now believe that the highly populated Southern California area is long overdue for a massive earthquake along the San Andreas fault.<ref>Rong-Gong Lin Il, Study shakes up scientists' view of San Andreas earthquake risk, http://www.latimes.com/news/local/la-me-earthquake-fault-20100821,0,946323.story?track=rss</ref>",
                    "#[[Image:Shelf in my reference library.jpg|thumb|200px|Beware of heavy books - place them on lower shelves|right]]Check for hazards inside your home and begin to fix them. There are a number of specific hazards in your home that you can deal with before an earthquake occurs.",
                    "#*[[Image:Wall Shelf.jpg|thumb|200px|Take care of precious ornaments by sticking them down|right]]Place breakable items (bottles, glass, china, etc.) in closed cabinets that have latches. Lock or latch them up so that the cabinet doors cannot fly open. Use poster tack/plastic putty to keep [[Collect Christmas Tree Ornaments|ornaments]], figurines, and [[Pack and Ship Your Fine China or Glassware|glassware]] adhered to shelves and mantelpieces. (There are even special commercial quake putties available.)",
                    "#*[[Image:Dsc_0001.jpg|right|180px|thumb|Make sure that the hot water heater is stable and cannot topple over]]Evaluate your electrical wiring, electrical appliances, and gas connections. Do any repairs if needed. During an earthquake, faulty fittings and wiring can become a potential [[Prevent a House Fire|fire hazard]]. When securing appliances, be sure not to drill holes in them - use existing holes, or make loops from leather, etc., that can be glued onto an appliance.",
                    "#[[Image:Quick, get under the table!.jpg|right|180px|thumb|Practice earthquake drills at home]]Create a Disaster Preparedness Plan. Know what you and your family are going to do before the earthquake happens. Form your plan together and go over it on a regular basis.",
                    "#*[[Image:Earthquake Strike Map San Francisco Bay Region 430.jpg|right|200px]]Try to develop routes and methods for getting home after an earthquake has occurred. Since there is no precise time of day when an earthquake might strike, you may be at work, at school, on a [[Ride a Public Transportation Bus|bus]], or in a train when one strikes. It is highly likely that you will need to know several ways to get home since roads and bridges will likely be obstructed for long periods of time.",
                    "#[[Image:What's in My Bag? UnPacked 2.jpg|right|180px|thumb|Prepare suitable supplies]][[Pack an Emergency Kit for the House|Prepare disaster supplies]] in advance. The following items are important to have readily available in your home, situated in a central location:",
                    "#Work with your community to be prepared. If there aren't civic groups present in your area focused on earthquake preparedness, work on putting one together. The first step in keeping everyone safe is education."
                ]
            }
        },
