            "id": "8539806",
            "user": "80.45.110.134",
            "timestamp": "10:35, 7 October 2012",
            "anon": true,
            "size": "(13,050 bytes)",
            "change": 0,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "adjustment",
            "reverted_by": "General Jackson",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*Decide on a rallying point for your family for after the earthquake. It should be away from buildings. Go over what your family should do in the event that not everyone makes it to the rallying point. If you have civil defence safety meeting points, be sure that every member of the family knows the location of the one closest to home, school, and work. Identify an out-of-area contact person that your family can call and get in touch with one another."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*Decide on a rallying point for your family for after the earthquake. It should be away from buildings. Go over what your family should do in the event that not everyone makes it to the rallying point. If you have civil defense safety meeting points, be sure that every member of the family knows the location of the one closest to home, school, and work. Identify an out-of-area contact person that your family can call and get in touch with one another."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "8463746",
            "user": "KizzaTomo",
            "timestamp": "10:06, 22 September 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(13,050 bytes)",
            "change": -558,
            "summary": "(Reverted edits by Eddie13 (Talk); changed back to last version by Genius_knight)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "{{fa}}[[Image:Paso Robles Earthquake 4744.jpg|right|250px]]An earthquake is a very dangerous natural disaster; particularly in the Pacific Rim region. In fact, scientists now believe that the highly populated Southern California area is long overdue for a massive earthquake along the San Andreas fault.<ref>Rong-Gong Lin Il, Study shakes up scientists' view of San Andreas earthquake risk, http://www.latimes.com/news/local/la-me-earthquake-fault-20100821,0,946323.story?track=rss</ref>",
                    "#[[Image:Shelf in my reference library.jpg|thumb|200px|Beware of heavy books - place them on lower shelves|right]]Check for hazards inside your home and begin to fix them. There are a number of specific hazards in your home that you can deal with before an earthquake occurs.",
                    "#*Fasten all shelves securely to the walls.",
                    "#*Install shatter-safe window films to protect from breaking glass. In a pinch, placing masking tape across the diagonals of your windows can keep them from shattering.",
                    "#* Place large, heavy objects on lower shelves or on the floor. They might fall during an earthquake and the less distance they have to fall, the better. You can also screw objects onto things, such as a desk.",
                    "#*Use some non-slip mats to prevent heavy objects, or objects with a low [[Calculate Center of Gravity|center of gravity]], from sliding. For example, fish bowls, vases, floral arrangements, statues, etc. Cut down to size if needed.",
                    "#*[[Image:Wall Shelf.jpg|thumb|200px|Take care of precious ornaments by sticking them down|right]]Place breakable items (bottles, glass, china, etc.) in closed cabinets that have latches. Lock or latch them up so that the cabinet doors cannot fly open. Use poster tack/plastic putty to keep [[Collect Christmas Tree Ornaments|ornaments]], figurines, and [[Pack and Ship Your Fine China or Glassware|glassware]] adhered to shelves and mantelpieces. (There are even special commercial quake putties available.)",
                    "#*Practice 'drop, cover and hold on.' Drop to the floor, take cover under a sturdy desk or table, and hold on firmly. Be prepared for shaking and falling objects."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "{{fa}}[[Image:Paso Robles Earthquake 4744.jpg|right|250px]]An earthquake is a natural occurence, often seen in the Pacific Rim region. In fact, scientists now believe that the highly populated Southern California area is long overdue for a massive earthquake along the San Andreas fault.<ref>Rong-Gong Lin Il, Study shakes up scientists' view of San Andreas earthquake risk, http://www.latimes.com/news/local/la-me-earthquake-fault-20100821,0,946323.story?track=rss</ref>",
                    "",
                    "Remember, an earthquake is no reason to panic or \"freak out\". They are perfectly natural and not dangerous.",
                    "#[[Image:Shelf in my reference library.jpg|thumb|200px|Place heavy books on higher shelves|right]]Check for hazards inside your home and begin to fix them. There are a number of specific hazards in your home that you can deal with before an earthquake occurs.",
                    "#*Install shatter-safe window films to protect from breaking glass. Keep masking tape across the diagonals of your windows at all times to keep them from shattering.",
                    "#* Place large, heavy objects on higher shelves to stabilize shelving units. You can also screw objects, like books and computers, onto things, such as a desk.",
                    "#*Use some wax paper to prevent heavy objects, or objects with a low [[Calculate Center of Gravity|center of gravity]], from sliding. For example, fish bowls, vases, floral arrangements, statues, etc. Cut down to size if needed.",
                    "#*[[Image:Wall Shelf.jpg|thumb|200px|Take care of precious ornaments by sticking them down|right]]Place breakable items (bottles, glass, china, etc.) in closed cabinets that have latches. Consider installing a heavy latch and padlock. Lock or latch them up so that the cabinet doors cannot fly open. Use poster tack/plastic putty to keep [[Collect Christmas Tree Ornaments|ornaments]], figurines, and [[Pack and Ship Your Fine China or Glassware|glassware]] adhered to shelves and mantelpieces. (There are even special commercial quake putties available.)",
                    "#  Dig a buffer zone underneath your house's foundation. You will need to dig a hole at the edge of your house until you reach the bottom of the foundation. Once you have your depth, dig underneath the foundation itself leave a three inch gap in between the foundation and the ground. Do this all around the house.",
                    "#  Keep plenty of candles around your home. During an earthquake try to avoid flashlights as the shaking is likely to shatter the bulbs or cause the batteries to leak acid. Candles are much safer.",
                    "#*Practice 'drop, cover and hold on.' When you see the flash drop to the floor, take cover under a sturdy desk or table, and hold on firmly. Be prepared for shaking and falling objects."
                ]
            }
        },
